abstract: >
  When a plot of land is converted into a token, a court order that prevents
  the sale of the property must become an action on the blockchain. Token
  standards for regulated assets, such as ERC-3643, already allow a token to
  be frozen, but they do not say who may trigger the freeze. Today this is left
  to a single administrative key, so whoever holds, misuses or steals that key
  can freeze or release land without a valid court order. We present
  CourtKeyRegistry, a smart contract that sits in front of the token's freeze
  function and accepts a freeze only when it can verify a valid court order on
  the blockchain. A registered judge, clerk and registrar must all sign the
  same order, their court must have territorial and pecuniary jurisdiction over
  the parcel, and the order must be bound to one parcel and case. Instead of
  trusting the caller, CourtKeyRegistry rebuilds the signed order digest itself
  and runs 12 checks before a freeze and 13 before a release. It is the only
  entity allowed to freeze the token, and every accepted order leaves a public
  record of who signed and which checks passed. We built a working prototype,
  NyayaChain, and deployed it on a four-validator Hyperledger Besu network
  using QBFT consensus. All 43 cases in our test suite pass on both a local
  Hardhat network and Besu. Across 100 three-signer freeze orders on Besu, the
  mean confirmation time was 1,994 ms, close to the 2 s block period.
